        'Signing in and the security check' => [
            'body' => '<p>The sign-in page is the first screen you see when you open the platform address in your browser. Anyone with an account uses it; accounts are created by an administrator under Administration &amp; support, Access &amp; Setup, Users &amp; Roles, and the first password is sent to you by email. Sign-in is protected by a security check (a CAPTCHA, an image of characters that a person can read but an automated program cannot) which is generated on the MAIIC server itself, so nothing about your visit is sent to any outside service.</p><h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul><li><b>Left panel</b> (wide screens only): the MAIIC logo, the strapline <b>Investing in Potential. Building Futures.</b>, the green, gold and red brand bar and the corporation\'s sector icons. It is decorative.</li><li><b>Sign-in card</b> with a green hexagon user badge, the heading <b>Welcome back</b> and the line <b>Sign in to access your account</b>.</li><li><b>Email address</b>: a box with an envelope icon and the prompt <b>Enter your email</b>.</li><li><b>Password</b>: a box with a padlock icon and the prompt <b>Enter your password</b>. The crossed-eye button at the right end (titled <b>Show password</b>, then <b>Hide password</b>) reveals or hides what you have typed.</li><li><b>Remember me</b>: a tick box that keeps you signed in on this browser after you close it.</li><li><b>Forgot password?</b>: a green link to the password reset page.</li><li><b>Security check</b>: an image showing five large green, slate and gold characters on a speckled background, a grey button with a circular-arrows icon titled <b>Get a new code</b>, and a box with the prompt <b>Enter the code shown above</b>. Under it the note <b>Type the characters shown above. Not case-sensitive.</b></li><li><b>Sign in</b>: the full-width green button with an arrow. While the server is checking it reads <b>Signing in...</b> and cannot be pressed twice.</li><li>A shield icon and the footer <b>Secure. Reliable. Committed to Growth.</b></li></ul><h4>Field by field</h4><ul><li><b>Email address</b>: the address your administrator registered, for example <code>analyst@maiic.mw</code>. Required.</li><li><b>Password</b>: your current password. Required. It is cleared after every attempt.</li><li><b>Security check</b>: the five characters in the image, typed in any case. The image never contains the digits 0 or 1 or the letters O, I or L, so there is no ambiguity. Each code is valid for five minutes and for one attempt only; a fresh image is drawn after every attempt and whenever you press <b>Get a new code</b>.</li></ul><h4>To reset a forgotten password</h4><ol><li>Press <b>Forgot password?</b> on the sign-in page.</li><li>Read the note: <b>Forgot your password? No problem. Just let us know your email address and we will email you a password reset link that will allow you to choose a new one.</b></li><li>Type your address in <b>Email</b> and press <b>Email Password Reset Link</b>.</li><li>Open the link in the email. On the reset page complete <b>Email</b>, <b>Password</b> and <b>Confirm Password</b> and press <b>Reset Password</b>.</li></ol><h4>If two-factor authentication is switched on</h4><p>After the password is accepted you are taken to a second page that says <b>Please confirm access to your account by entering the authentication code provided by your authenticator application.</b> Type the six-digit number from your phone in <b>Code</b> and press <b>Log in</b>. If your phone is not to hand, press <b>Use a recovery code</b>, type one of your saved recovery codes in <b>Recovery Code</b> and press <b>Log in</b>; <b>Use an authentication code</b> switches back. Each recovery code works once.</p><h4>What happens next</h4><p>On success you land on the IFRS 9 ECL Dashboard. Your sign-in is recorded against your account and appears under Browser Sessions on your Profile page.</p><h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>The security code is incorrect or has expired. Please enter the new code shown.</b> The characters did not match, or more than five minutes passed. A new image has already been drawn; type the new code and try again.</li><li><b>These credentials do not match our records.</b> The email or password is wrong. Check for a stale saved password in your browser, then use <b>Forgot password?</b> if needed.</li><li><b>Too many attempts.</b> After five failed attempts from the same address within one minute the server refuses further attempts for a short while (error code 429). Wait a minute and try again.</li><li><b>The code image is blank.</b> Press <b>Get a new code</b>. If it stays blank, tell your administrator: the image library on the server may be missing.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open the platform address in your browser.',
                'Type your registered address in Email address.',
                'Type your password in Password. Press the eye button if you want to check what you typed.',
                'Tick Remember me if this is your own computer and you want to stay signed in.',
                'Read the five characters in the Security check image and type them in the box below it. Press the circular-arrows button (Get a new code) if the image is hard to read.',
                'Press Sign in. If two-factor authentication is on, enter the code from your phone and press Log in.',
                'You land on the IFRS 9 ECL Dashboard.',
            ],
            'images' => ['login' => 'The sign-in page: email, password with show and hide button, Remember me, Forgot password?, the security check image with its refresh button, and the Sign in button'],
            'routes' => ['login', 'password.request', 'password.reset', 'two-factor.login'],
        ],

        'Your profile, password and two-factor authentication' => [
            'body' => '<p>The Profile page is where you look after your own account: your name, email address and photo, your password, two-factor authentication, the browsers you are signed in on, and account deletion. Every signed-in user has one. To reach it, press the round avatar at the far right of the top header and choose <b>Profile</b> from the menu.</p><h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul><li>Page title <b>Profile</b>. There is no help button on this page.</li><li><b>Profile Information</b>: the panel on the right has <b>Photo</b> (your current picture, or a coloured circle with your initial), the button <b>SELECT A NEW PHOTO</b>, <b>Remove Photo</b> (only once a photo has been uploaded), the fields <b>Name</b> and <b>Email</b>, and a green <b>SAVE</b> button. A small <b>Saved.</b> message appears beside the button after a successful save.</li><li><b>Update Password</b>: <b>Current Password</b>, <b>New Password</b>, <b>Confirm Password</b> and <b>SAVE</b>, again with <b>Saved.</b> on success.</li><li><b>Two Factor Authentication</b>: a heading reading either <b>You have not enabled two factor authentication.</b> or <b>You have enabled two factor authentication.</b>, an explanation, and the buttons <b>Enable</b> (when off) or <b>Regenerate Recovery Codes</b>, <b>Show Recovery Codes</b> and the red <b>Disable</b> (when on). After enabling, a QR code and a grey box of recovery codes are shown.</li><li><b>Browser Sessions</b>: a list of recent sign-ins, each showing platform and browser, the network address, and either <b>This device</b> in green or <b>Last active</b> followed by a time. Below it the button <b>Log Out Other Browser Sessions</b> and the message <b>Done.</b> after success.</li><li><b>Delete Account</b>: a warning and a red <b>Delete Account</b> button.</li></ul><h4>Field by field</h4><ul><li><b>Photo</b>: any image file; it is shown as a circle in the header. Optional.</li><li><b>Name</b>: your display name as it appears on tickets, workspace messages and the audit trail. Required.</li><li><b>Email</b>: your sign-in address. Required and must be unique.</li><li><b>Current Password</b>: required; the new password is refused if it is wrong.</li><li><b>New Password</b> and <b>Confirm Password</b>: at least eight characters and both boxes must match.</li></ul><h4>To change your password</h4><ol><li>Under <b>Update Password</b>, type your existing password in <b>Current Password</b>.</li><li>Type the new password in <b>New Password</b> and again in <b>Confirm Password</b>.</li><li>Press <b>SAVE</b> and wait for <b>Saved.</b></li></ol><h4>To switch on two-factor authentication</h4><ol><li>Install an authenticator app on your phone (for example Google Authenticator).</li><li>Under <b>Two Factor Authentication</b> press <b>Enable</b>.</li><li>A <b>Confirm Password</b> box appears with the text <b>For your security, please confirm your password to continue.</b> Type your password and press <b>Confirm</b> (or <b>Nevermind</b> to stop).</li><li>Scan the QR code with the app. The heading changes to <b>Two factor authentication is now enabled. Scan the following QR code using your phone\'s authenticator application.</b></li><li>Copy the recovery codes from the grey box into a password manager or another safe place; the page says <b>Store these recovery codes in a secure password manager.</b></li></ol><p>From now on every sign-in asks for the six-digit code after your password. <b>Regenerate Recovery Codes</b> replaces the codes with a new set (the old ones stop working); <b>Show Recovery Codes</b> displays the current set; <b>Disable</b> switches the feature off. Each of these asks for your password first.</p><h4>To sign out of other devices</h4><ol><li>Under <b>Browser Sessions</b> press <b>Log Out Other Browser Sessions</b>.</li><li>Type your password in the box and press <b>Log Out Other Browser Sessions</b> in the dialogue, or <b>Nevermind</b> to cancel.</li></ol><h4>What happens next</h4><p>A new name is used immediately across the platform. A changed password takes effect on your next sign-in. Deleting your account (<b>Delete Account</b>, then your password, then <b>Delete Account</b> in the dialogue) removes your user permanently; records you created, such as tickets and messages, keep your name as text.</p><h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>The provided password does not match your current password.</b> Retype <b>Current Password</b>.</li><li><b>The password confirmation does not match.</b> The two new-password boxes differ.</li><li><b>The email has already been taken.</b> Another user already uses that address.</li><li><b>I lost my phone.</b> Sign in with a recovery code, then press <b>Disable</b> and <b>Enable</b> again to pair a new phone. If you have no codes left, an administrator must reset your account.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Press the round avatar at the top right of any page and choose Profile.',
                'Under Profile Information, change Name or Email, or press SELECT A NEW PHOTO to upload a picture, then press SAVE.',
                'Under Update Password, complete Current Password, New Password and Confirm Password and press SAVE.',
                'Under Two Factor Authentication, press Enable, confirm your password, scan the QR code with your authenticator app and store the recovery codes.',
                'Under Browser Sessions, press Log Out Other Browser Sessions if you have signed in on a shared computer.',
            ],
            'images' => ['profile' => 'The Profile page: Profile Information with photo, name and email, followed by Update Password'],
            'routes' => ['profile.show'],
        ],

        'The screen layout' => [
            'body' => '<p>Every page after sign-in shares one frame: the dark green sidebar on the left, a white top bar, and the page below it, which always starts with a breadcrumb, the page title, any tabs, and a row of the page\'s buttons above its table. Learning the frame once makes every module familiar. This article names each part; the next covers the controls inside pages.</p>'
                . '<h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul>'
                . '<li><b>Sidebar</b>: see Finding your way.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Top bar, Search box</b>: a box with a magnifier and the prompt <b>Search</b>. In this release it does not search across the platform; use the search box of each list instead.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Top bar, notification bell</b>: grey when nothing is new; amber, swinging, with a red count when you have unread notifications. See Notifications.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Top bar, avatar</b>: your photo or initial in a circle. It opens a menu with <b>Profile</b> and <b>Log Out</b>.</li>'
                . '<li><b>No theme switch</b>: the platform is always shown in light mode; there is no appearance button in the top bar or on the Profile page.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Breadcrumb</b>: the page\'s place in the sidebar, each crumb a link back up (see Finding your way).</li>'
                . '<li><b>Page heading</b>: the title in bold with a one-line description in grey beneath it. On some screens a small round green help button sits beside the title.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Tab row</b>: when a sidebar entry holds several screens, their tabs sit in a white panel under the heading (for example Users and Roles &amp; Permissions). The active tab is green with a green underline. A chip beside a tab gives the number of records that tab lists (green on the active tab, grey on the others). Each tab has its own address, so a bookmark opens that tab.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Actions row</b>: the page\'s buttons (for example <b>Add user</b>, <b>New ticket</b>, <b>Export CSV</b>, or the Dashboard filter bar and <b>PDF</b>) sit together at the far right, directly above the page\'s first table, below the tabs, tiles and filters they act on. On a page without a table the row sits under the tab row. When you switch a tab inside the page, the row moves to sit above the table now shown.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Messages after an action</b>, under the tab row: a green box with a tick titled <b>Done</b>; a red box with a cross titled <b>Something went wrong</b>; a red box titled <b>Please correct one thing before saving</b> or <b>Please correct N things before saving</b> listing each reason when a form is refused; an amber box with a warning triangle titled <b>Please note</b>; a blue-grey box titled <b>For your information</b>. Each has an X (<b>Close this message</b>); a new action brings messages back.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Loading pill</b>: when a page takes more than a moment, a gold pill with a spinning circle and <b>Loading</b> appears at the top centre; when the page arrives it turns green with a tick and <b>Done</b> for a moment. Pages that load at once show nothing.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Help button</b>: a green round button with a question mark (<b>Help for this screen</b>), either small beside the title or floating at the bottom right. It opens this User Manual at the article written for the screen. See Getting help.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>The error dialogue</h4><p>If an action cannot be completed the platform never shows a raw error page. A white dialogue with a red warning icon explains what happened, shows <b>Error code</b> where one applies, and offers a green <b>OK, got it</b> button:</p><ul>'
                . '<li><b>No access rights</b>: You do not have the rights to view this page or perform this action. Contact your administrator if you believe you should have access. (code 403)</li>'
                . '<li><b>Session expired</b>: Your session has expired. Refresh the page and sign in again, then retry. (code 419)</li>'
                . '<li><b>Not found</b>: The item you tried to open no longer exists. Refresh and try again. (code 404)</li>'
                . '<li><b>Action could not be completed</b>: The file or request is too large to process. (413); Too many requests in a short time. Wait a moment and try again. (429); The system is temporarily unavailable. Please try again shortly. (503); The server hit an error while processing this action. It has been logged. (500 and above); otherwise The server could not complete this action. Nothing was changed.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Connection problem</b>: Could not reach the server. Check your connection and try again. Nothing was saved.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>On a phone or narrow window</h4><p>The sidebar becomes a drawer opened with the three-line button (<b>Open sidebar</b>) at the left of the top bar. Wide tables scroll sideways inside their own panel rather than the whole page, and the breadcrumb folds its middle crumbs into "...".</p>'
                . '<h4>To sign out</h4><ol><li>Press the avatar at the top right.</li><li>Choose <b>Log Out</b>. You return to the sign-in page.</li></ol>'
                . '<h4>What happens next</h4><p>Nothing in the frame changes data by itself; only the page content does. The top bar is the same on every page.</p>'
                . '<h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>The page is stuck on Loading.</b> The server has not answered. Wait, then refresh the browser; if the <b>Connection problem</b> dialogue appears, check your network.</li><li><b>Session expired keeps appearing.</b> You were signed out, for example after a long idle period. Refresh the page and sign in again.</li><li><b>A tab or button answers No access rights.</b> The sidebar hides what your role cannot open, but a tab or a link inside a page can still lead to a screen that needs a further permission. Ask an administrator.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Find the page in the sidebar, or press a crumb of the breadcrumb to go back up.',
                'Use the tab row under the title to move between the screens of one entry.',
                'Look for the page\'s buttons in the actions row at the right, directly above the table.',
                'Read any message box under the tabs, then press its X to close it.',
                'If a dialogue with a red warning icon appears, read it, press OK, got it, and follow its advice.',
                'Press the bell for notifications and the avatar for Profile or Log Out.',
            ],
            'images' => ['workspace' => 'The shared frame: sidebar, top bar with Search, bell and avatar, breadcrumb, page title, tabs and the actions row at the right'],
        ],

        'Common controls and icons' => [
            'body' => '<p>The platform uses one design system, so a button, badge or icon means the same on every page. This article is the key to it. Status colours carry meaning: green is positive or complete, gold is pending or a warning, red is adverse or destructive, grey is neutral or inactive.</p>'
                . '<h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul>'
                . '<li><b>Panels</b>: white cards with a soft border hold every table and form.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Tables</b>: a dark green header band with white capitals; alternate rows shaded light grey; the row under the pointer turns pale green. Number columns are right-aligned. A totals row is bold on grey with a gold line above it. When there is nothing to show, a grey line or a short heading with advice says so, for example <b>No users match. Clear the search, or add a user.</b></li>'
                . '<li><b>Search and filter row</b>: a slim row at the top of a table panel with a search box whose prompt names what it searches (for example <b>Search name or email</b> or <b>Search reference or title</b>), drop-downs such as <b>All roles</b>, <b>All categories</b> or <b>All users</b>, sometimes <b>From</b> and <b>To</b> date boxes, and a <b>Clear</b> link once anything is set. A count on the right gives the number of records, for example <b>12 users</b>. The list reloads by itself a moment after you stop typing or choose. Some older screens instead have a <b>Filter</b> button with a small triangle that opens a panel of drop-downs, a <b>Search...</b> box and a <b>Reset</b> link.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Tabs inside a page</b>: the same look as the section tabs: the active tab green with an underline and a count chip beside each.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Column sort</b>: where a table can be sorted (for example Product Groups), each column heading is a button titled <b>Sort by</b> and the column name, with a small sort icon. Press it to sort ascending, again for descending; the active column\'s icon is solid. Other tables keep the order the page states (often newest first).</li>'
                . '<li><b>Row action icons</b> at the right of each row, each a small rounded square: green eye (<b>View</b>), gold pencil (<b>Edit</b>), red bin (<b>Delete</b>), grey icons for neutral actions (for example a padlock to close a financial period or a download arrow for failed rows). On rows that wait for a second person: a green tick (<b>Approve</b>), a red cross (<b>Reject</b>), a gold no-entry sign (<b>Withdraw</b>, for the proposer) and a gold circular arrow (<b>Reverse</b>). A green arrow (<b>Open this screen</b>) goes to another screen. Hover any icon for its exact title. An icon appears only when your role may use it, and an approve button is never offered to the person who proposed the item.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Badges</b>: rounded pills. Light green means active, done or favourable; solid green means complete or resolved; gold means pending, on watch or high priority; red means adverse, critical or an error; grey means inactive, closed, locked or system-set.</li>'
                . '<li><b>KPI tiles</b>: white cards with a coloured left edge, a small capital label, a value and, where relevant, a small exact amount, a progress bar or a change chip. Hover a tile for the full figure.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Chart controls</b>: a <b>Chart</b> / <b>Table</b> switch on each chart panel (the green half is the view shown; Table shows the same figures as rows you can copy); range drop-downs <b>Last 12 months</b>, <b>From the start</b> or <b>From</b> a month, and <b>To</b> a month; a legend under the chart, where pressing an entry hides or shows that series; tooltips with exact figures when you hover.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Ties badges</b>: a green badge such as <b>Ties to the total ECL</b> says two sources of the same figure agree; a gold one such as <b>Differs from the saved runs</b> says they do not, and hovering it gives the amounts.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Export buttons</b>: a download or file icon and a label naming the file (<b>Export CSV</b>, <b>Download CSV</b>, <b>Excel</b>, <b>PDF</b>, <b>Download PDF</b>). The file reflects the filters on screen and downloads at once. A few exports first open a dialogue (for example <b>Export Options</b>, <b>Export Format</b>), whose button reads <b>Exporting...</b> until the file is ready.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Confirmation dialogue</b>: a white box with an icon in a circle (a red bin for a delete, an amber triangle for an action that is hard to undo, a green circle for a plain confirmation), the question as its title, an explanation, a white <b>Cancel</b> button and a button naming the action (red for a delete, for example <b>Delete ticket</b> or <b>Close period</b>). <b>Cancel</b> has the focus, so Enter never deletes by accident. Escape, Cancel or a click outside the box cancels. A destructive maintenance job also asks you to type a word before its button works.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Reason box</b>: an action that must be explained (reject, reverse, lock, unlock, tie) opens a box with a <b>Reason</b> field (usually 10 to 500 characters), <b>Cancel</b> and an action button that stays grey until a reason is typed. The reason is written to the audit log.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Pagination</b>: under long lists, numbered pages with the current page in solid green and <b>Previous</b> and <b>Next</b> at the ends; greyed buttons are unavailable. Most lists show fifteen rows a page; some add <b>Showing 1 to 15 of 230</b>. With only one page there is no pager.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Forms</b>: field labels in small grey capitals above each box; section titles in small green capitals with a gold underline; a refused field shows its reason in red underneath.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Locks</b>: a locked record or month shows a grey <b>Locked</b> badge or a padlock. A locked EIR or reporting period is final: it is reopened only by a deliberate, recorded action with a reason (for example <b>Reopen locked EIR</b>, or Unlock on Periods Built).</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>How numbers are written</h4><ul><li>Money is shown in the organisation currency set in Settings, with thousands separators and two decimals: <code>384,449,701.57</code>.</li><li>Negative amounts are shown in parentheses: <code>(384,449,701.57)</code>.</li><li>Large KPI values are compacted: <code>384.45M</code>, <code>66.40B</code>, <code>950.0K</code>; the exact amount is printed under them.</li><li>Percentages carry two decimals: <code>1.00%</code>. Changes are a percentage for money and points (<code>pts</code>) for ratios.</li><li>Months are written <code>2025-11</code> or <b>Nov 2025</b>. A dash (-) in a cell means there is no value, never a zero.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>To filter a list</h4><ol><li>Type in the search box or choose values in the drop-downs.</li><li>The table refreshes by itself a moment later.</li><li>Press <b>Clear</b> (or <b>Reset</b>) to remove every filter.</li></ol>'
                . '<h4>What happens next</h4><p>Filters, sorting, tabs and chart switches change only what is shown; they never change data. Saves, deletes, approvals and exports act on records and are written to the Audit Trail.</p>'
                . '<h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>The eye, pencil or bin is missing on a row.</b> Your role lacks the matching permission, or the record is locked.</li><li><b>Approve is missing on an item I can see.</b> You proposed it.</li><li><b>Numbers look a thousand times too small.</b> Read the exact figure under a compacted value, or switch the panel to Table.</li><li><b>A total is in parentheses.</b> It is negative.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Read a badge by its colour: green is good or done, gold is pending or on watch, red is adverse or an error, grey is neutral, inactive or locked.',
                'Use the row icons: green eye to view, gold pencil to edit, red bin to delete; a confirmation always follows a delete.',
                'Filter a list with its search box and drop-downs; press Clear to start again.',
                'Find the page buttons, such as exports, in the actions row above the table.',
                'Switch a chart to Table when you need the exact figures, and use From and To to change its range.',
                'Read amounts as two-decimal accounting numbers; parentheses mean negative and M or B on a tile means millions or billions.',
            ],
            'images' => ['tickets' => 'A standard list: status tabs with counts, the search and filter row, coloured badges and the green eye, gold pencil and red bin row icons'],
        ],

        'Importing files: sample file, column guide and Check file' => [
            'body' => '<p>Every file import in the platform shows the same instructions panel: clients, the loan book (standard layout and E-Banker report), collateral, credit loss data, the EIR intake files, trial balances, the take-on workbooks, the E-Banker pack, the S&amp;P internal grades workbook, SICR groups and SICR alert items, and the macro series. The panel, the sample file you download, the column guide and the check the server runs before anything is saved all come from one register of import specifications, so they cannot disagree. The panel sits on the <b>Upload</b> tab of an import screen, or inside the import dialogue on screens such as SICR Groups and SICR Alert Items. Anyone who can open an import screen sees it; the import itself needs that screen\'s own permission.</p>'
                . '<h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul>'
                . '<li>A pale green panel headed <b>How to import</b> in small capitals, the import\'s title (for example <b>Clients</b> or <b>Loan book (standard layout)</b>) and a one-line summary of what one row means.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Numbered steps</b> in green circles: the instructions for this import, then any steps particular to the screen, then grey notes where the import has them.</li>'
                . '<li>A link with a small chevron: <b>Hide the N columns (M required)</b> or <b>Show the N columns (M required)</b>; for a pack or workbook, <b>Show the N files and sheets read</b>. The table opens by itself when the import has 14 columns or fewer.</li>'
                . '<li><b>The column table</b>: <b>Column</b> (the exact heading to use), <b>Type</b>, <b>Required</b> (a green <b>Required</b> tag, or <b>Optional</b>), <b>Format and allowed values</b>, <b>Description</b> and <b>Example</b>. A file read by position adds a first column <b>Position</b>. For a pack or workbook the table lists <b>File or sheet</b>, <b>Required</b> (<b>Required</b> or <b>When delivered</b>) and <b>What it holds</b>.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Buttons</b> at the right of the panel: <b>Check file</b>, <b>Download sample file</b> (or <b>Download sample manifest</b>) and <b>Download column guide</b>.</li>'
                . '<li>Below the panel, the screen\'s own fields: the file box (for example <b>Choose a CSV or Excel file</b>), any choice the import needs (the portfolio, the month-end, the file layout, Match columns) and the import button.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Buttons and icons</h4><ul>'
                . '<li><b>Download sample file</b> (download arrow): the sample in exactly the format the import reads, with the headings and illustrative rows, named for example <code>clients_sample.csv</code> (hover the button for the name). Start every new file from it. The E-Banker pack offers <b>Download sample manifest</b> instead; imports with no blank template (the take-on and S&amp;P workbooks, produced by Finance) have no sample button.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Download column guide</b> (document icon): an Excel workbook named for example <code>clients_column_guide.xlsx</code> with the sheets <b>Instructions</b> (summary, file type, steps, required columns, notes), <b>Columns</b> or <b>Files</b>, and <b>Sample</b>.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Check file</b> (tick icon; hover text <b>Read the file and list every problem by row. Nothing is saved.</b>): appears once a file is chosen, for imports that can be checked on their own. The server reads the file the way the import would and tests the headings (every required column present, no column twice) and every row (a value in each required cell, the type, the allowed values, the range, no key repeated). It reads <b>Checking...</b> while it works. Nothing is written.</li>'
                . '<li><b>The import button</b>, named on each screen (for example <b>Start import</b>, which reads <b>Uploading...</b> while the file is sent). The server runs the same check again before anything is written.</li>'
                . '<li><b>Close</b> on a check result hides it.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>The check result</h4><ul>'
                . '<li>A green tick: <b>The file passed the check</b>. Amber: <b>The file passed the check, with warnings</b>. Red: <b>The file has problems: fix the rows below before importing</b> (after Check file) or <b>The file was refused: fix the rows below and choose it again</b> (after an import was refused).</li>'
                . '<li>A line with the file name, <b>N rows read, N errors, N warnings.</b> and <b>Check only: nothing was saved.</b> or <b>Nothing was saved.</b>; then <b>Columns not read:</b> and any headings the import ignores.</li>'
                . '<li>A table of problems: <b>Row</b>, <b>Column</b>, <b>Problem</b> (a red <b>Error</b> or amber <b>Warning</b> tag and the message) and <b>How to fix it</b>. Up to 200 are listed; beyond that the panel says <b>The first 200 problems are listed. Fix these and check the file again to see the rest.</b></li></ul>'
                . '<h4>To import a file</h4><ol><li>Open the import screen and its <b>Upload</b> tab (or the import dialogue).</li><li>Read the steps and the column table, then press <b>Download sample file</b> and start from it.</li><li>Fill one row per record, keep the headings, and write each value in the format the table shows.</li><li>Choose the file and any options the screen asks for.</li><li>Press <b>Check file</b> and fix every row listed until the result reads <b>The file passed the check</b>.</li><li>Press the import button, then follow the import on the <b>History</b> tab or on Imports.</li></ol>'
                . '<h4>What happens next</h4><p>The check fails closed: an error on any row refuses the whole file and nothing is saved, so a half-loaded file never reaches the engines. Warnings (a value the import will fold, or a repeat it is built to skip) are listed but do not refuse the file. A clean file is imported; on screens such as Clients and Loan Book the import then runs in the background, and the <b>History</b> tab lists each upload with <b>File name</b>, <b>Status</b> (completed green, processing gold, pending grey, failed red), <b>Uploaded</b>, <b>Records inserted</b>, <b>Failed rows</b> and, when rows failed while processing, a grey download button (<b>Download the failed rows</b>). <b>Open the full import history</b> goes to the Imports tab. Imports are written to the audit trail.</p>'
                . '<h4>Empty states and messages</h4><ul><li><b>Loading the import instructions...</b> while the panel loads.</li><li><b>The import instructions could not be loaded. Refresh the page to try again.</b></li><li><b>The file could not be checked.</b> (or the server\'s reason) in red under the panel.</li><li>A refused import shows a red message: the file name, <b>was not imported:</b> the number of problems and the first one, then <b>Nothing was saved. Fix the rows listed and choose the file again.</b></li><li><b>The file is empty: it has no heading row.</b> or <b>The file could not be read:</b> with the advice <b>Save the file in the format of the sample file and choose it again.</b></li><li><b>No file has been imported yet. Use the Upload tab to import the first one.</b> on an empty History tab.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>There is no Check file button.</b> Choose a file first. The take-on workbooks, the E-Banker pack and the S&amp;P workbook check themselves through their own <b>Check only</b> or landing step.</li><li><b>A column is listed under Columns not read.</b> Its heading differs from the sample; rename it, or use Match columns where the screen offers it.</li><li><b>Leading zeros vanished from customer numbers.</b> Save the column as text before importing.</li><li><b>The file is too large.</b> A file over 50 MB cannot be checked; split it by month.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'Open the import screen and its Upload tab.',
                'Read the numbered steps and the column table in the How to import panel.',
                'Press Download sample file and build your file from it, keeping the headings.',
                'Press Download column guide when you need every column\'s format and allowed values in Excel.',
                'Choose your file and press Check file; fix every row listed until the file passes.',
                'Press the import button and follow the upload on the History tab.',
            ],
            'images' => ['import-modal' => 'The How to import panel: numbered steps, the column table with Required tags, and the Check file, Download sample file and Download column guide buttons'],
        ],

        'Getting help' => [
            'body' => '<p>Help is built into the platform in three layers: a help button on many screens, four documents under <b>System Documentation</b> in the sidebar, and Support Tickets for anything the documents do not answer. Every signed-in user can use all three.</p>'
                . '<h4>What you see on the screen</h4><ul>'
                . '<li><b>Help button</b>: a green round button with a white question mark (hover text <b>Help for this screen</b>). On some screens it sits small beside the page title (for example the Dashboard, Clients and ECL Calculation); on others it floats at the bottom right (for example Loan Book, Portfolios, Settings and the PD and LGD screens). Pressing it opens this User Manual at the article written for that screen, or at the manual\'s contents when no article is linked to the screen yet.</li>'
                . '<li><b>System Documentation</b>, under <b>Administration &amp; support</b>, <b>Support &amp; Maintenance</b>, with four entries: <b>User Manual</b>, <b>Administrator Manual</b>, <b>Technical Manual</b> and <b>Installation Guide</b>.</li>'
                . '<li>Each manual page has the title, a line giving the number of chapters and topics, <b>Download PDF</b> (green, file icon) and, for users with the <code>settings</code> permission, <b>Edit manual</b> (pencil). On the left a contents rail with a <b>Search this manual...</b> box, <b>Cover and contents</b> and the chapters, each with its number of topics; pressing a chapter lists its topics. On a narrow screen a chapter drop-down replaces the rail.</li>'
                . '<li>The cover shows the organisation logo, the document name and a document control block. Each article shows its title, an <b>Updated</b> date, the text, numbered steps in green circles and screenshots with captions; press a screenshot to enlarge it and press anywhere to close it. At the foot of a chapter, buttons name the previous and next chapters, with <b>Chapter N of M</b> between them.</li>'
                . '<li>The Technical Manual and Installation Guide are kept with the software rather than edited on screen; the Technical Manual ends with a live data dictionary read from the database.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Which document to read</h4><ul><li><b>User Manual</b>: day-to-day use for analysts and reviewers. This document.</li><li><b>Administrator Manual</b>: users and access, settings, periods and locks, imports and feeds, maintenance and support.</li><li><b>Technical Manual</b>: architecture, data model, calculation engines, security and operations, for ICT staff and auditors.</li><li><b>Installation Guide</b>: installing and configuring the platform in the MAIIC environment.</li></ul>'
                . '<h4>To find an answer in a manual</h4><ol><li>Press the help button on the screen you are on, or open <b>System Documentation</b> and choose <b>User Manual</b>.</li><li>Type a word in <b>Search this manual...</b>; the rail and the articles shrink to those that mention it.</li><li>Press a topic in the rail to jump to it.</li><li>Press <b>Download PDF</b> to save the whole manual as a branded PDF with the same content.</li></ol>'
                . '<h4>To raise an issue or request a change</h4><ol><li>Open <b>Administration &amp; support</b>, <b>Support &amp; Maintenance</b>, <b>Support Tickets</b>.</li><li>Press <b>New ticket</b>, describe the problem or request, and press <b>Create ticket</b>. See Support tickets for the full procedure.</li></ol>'
                . '<h4>What happens next</h4><p>Reading help changes nothing. The manuals are content in the database, so when an administrator edits an article the change shows on screen and in the next PDF at once.</p>'
                . '<h4>Empty states and messages</h4><ul><li><b>Nothing in this manual matches</b> and your search word: try a shorter word.</li><li><b>This manual has no published content yet</b> with <b>An administrator adds chapters and topics with Edit manual.</b></li></ul>'
                . '<h4>Common problems</h4><ul><li><b>The help button opened the contents, not an article.</b> No article is linked to that screen yet; use the search box.</li><li><b>There is no help button on a screen.</b> Not every screen carries one; open the User Manual from System Documentation and search.</li><li><b>Download PDF is slow.</b> The PDF holds the whole manual with every screenshot; allow a short wait.</li></ul>',
            'steps' => [
                'On a screen with a green question-mark button, press it to open the manual at that screen\'s article.',
                'Otherwise open Administration & support, Support & Maintenance, System Documentation and choose User Manual.',
                'Type a word in Search this manual... and press a topic in the left rail.',
                'Press Download PDF to keep an offline copy.',
                'If the manuals do not answer your question, open Support Tickets and press New ticket.',
            ],
            'images' => [
                'help' => 'The User Manual: contents rail with search, the Edit manual and Download PDF buttons, and the cover page',
                'help-admin' => 'The Administrator Manual uses the same reader',
                'docs-technical' => 'The Technical Manual',
                'docs-installation' => 'The Installation Guide',
            ],
            'routes' => ['help.index', 'help.admin', 'docs.technical', 'docs.installation'],
        ],
    ],
];
